# Day 07 – Linux File System Hierarchy & Scenario-Based Practice

# Part 1 – Linux File System Hierarchy

The Linux filesystem is organized in a hierarchical structure starting from the root directory `/`.

---

## 1. `/` – Root Directory

### Purpose

* The root directory is the starting point of the Linux filesystem.
* All files and directories are located under `/`.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /
```

### Example Output

```text
bin
boot
dev
etc
home
opt
root
tmp
usr
var
```

### I would use this when...

I need to navigate the Linux filesystem or locate important system directories.

---

## 2. `/home`

### Purpose

* Stores the home directories of normal users.
* Each normal user generally has a personal home directory here.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /home
```

### Example Output

```text
ubuntu
john
```

### I would use this when...

I need to access user files or troubleshoot user-specific issues.

---

## 3. `/root`

### Purpose

* Home directory of the `root` administrator account.
* Normally accessible only to the root user.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /root
```

### Example Output

```text
scripts
backup.sh
```

### I would use this when...

Managing administrative files, scripts, or root-user configuration.

---

## 4. `/etc`

### Purpose

* Contains system-wide configuration files.
* Many Linux services and applications store configuration under `/etc`.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /etc
```

### Example Output

```text
hosts
hostname
passwd
ssh
systemd
```

### I would use this when...

Checking or troubleshooting Linux and service configuration files.

---

## 5. `/var/log`

### Purpose

* Contains log files generated by the operating system, services, and applications.
* One of the most important locations for troubleshooting.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /var/log
```

### Example Output

```text
syslog
auth.log
kern.log
journal
```

### I would use this when...

Checking logs to diagnose system, service, or application issues.

---

## 6. `/tmp`

### Purpose

* Used for temporary files and directories.
* Temporary data may be cleaned automatically depending on the system configuration.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /tmp
```

### Example Output

```text
systemd-private
tempfile.txt
```

### I would use this when...

Creating temporary files or directories during testing and troubleshooting.

---

## 7. `/bin`

### Purpose

* Traditionally contains essential user command binaries.
* On many modern Linux distributions, `/bin` is a symbolic link to `/usr/bin`.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /bin
```

### Example Output

```text
bash
cp
mv
ls
cat
```

### I would use this when...

Understanding where commonly used system command binaries are located.

---

## 8. `/usr/bin`

### Purpose

* Contains a large collection of executable programs and utilities available to users.
* On modern Linux systems, many standard commands and installed applications are located here.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /usr/bin
```

### Example Output

```text
python3
git
vim
```

### I would use this when...

Locating installed commands and executable programs.

---

## 9. `/opt`

### Purpose

* Commonly used for optional or third-party software.
* Software installed outside the normal package-managed locations may be placed here.

### Command

```bash
ls -l /opt
```

### Example Output

```text
google
microsoft
```

### I would use this when...

Installing or managing third-party applications.

